<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.26100.0</TargetFramework>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>10.0.19041.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <RootNamespace>Juice.Cli</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>juice</AssemblyName>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;win-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <!--
      Region and currency resolution depend on real globalization data: invariant mode
      makes RegionInfo.CurrentRegion useless, which breaks electricity rate lookup.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>
    <!--
      ReadyToRun is on, matching Juice.App, and it has to be a package-wide decision rather
      than a per-project one.

      An MSIX layout is a single flat folder. The tray application's build supplies every
      shared assembly in it, and pack.ps1 copies only four files out of this project's
      publish on top: juice.exe, juice.dll and the two json files. Of those, only juice.dll
      contains IL, so this setting decides the fate of exactly one assembly in the package.

      That assembly costs 50,176 bytes to precompile, 27,648 against 77,824. Set against
      the 12.6 MB the package already spends precompiling on arm64, declining to spend it
      would leave the only code unique to the command line as the single assembly still
      jitted, surrounded by precompiled ones, and save four hundredths of a percent of the
      package for the trouble.

      Measured in isolation the tool gains little from precompiling, because it is not
      startup bound: median wall time for juice now was 2040 ms against 2050 ms over twelve
      interleaved runs of each, with the ranges overlapping. That measurement argues about
      whether the package should use ReadyToRun at all, which is Juice.App's decision, and
      it does. Once that is paid for, excluding one executable from it buys nothing.

      Note that publishing this project standalone with it on costs 43 MB, the Windows API
      projection growing from 25 MB to 63 MB, which pack.ps1 pays for and then discards
      since it copies only four files. That is packaging time, not package size.
    -->
    <PublishReadyToRun>true</PublishReadyToRun>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Juice.Core\Juice.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Juice.Platform.Windows\Juice.Platform.Windows.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
